    "available_at": "2026-10-28T08:06:56.470817+00:00",
    "body": "Truckload broker Landstar System noted strong demand for its dry van and flatbed offerings on its third-quarter call with analysts on Tuesday. A tightened capacity backdrop and the buildout of data centers are shaping the favorable fundamentals. However, insurance claims activity clouded an otherwise solid result.\n“You can look down the P&L and if you skip one line known as insurance and claims, you’d have a lot of good news to write home about,” said Landstar President and CEO Frank Lonegro.\nLandstar ( NASDAQ: LSTR ) reported third-quarter earnings per share of $1.42, which was 24 cents below the consensus estimate. The miss was attributable to $13 million (29 cents per share) in adverse adjustments to claims reserves and $8 million (18 cents per share) in unfavorable developments on two prior-year claims.\nThe result was 20 cents better than the adjusted EPS result from a year ago, which excluded several non-recurring items .\nTable: Landstar’s key performance indicators\nConsolidated revenue was up 25% y/y to $1.51 billion, which was largely in line with the consensus estimate.\nTotal truck revenue increased 29% y/y to $1.41 billion as loads increased 3% and revenue per load jumped 25%. Higher diesel fuel prices, along with improving TL pricing, drove the yield increase. Revenue per load was up 7.5% from the second quarter .\nDry van loads increased 6% y/y, with revenue per load up 23%. Flatbed loads increased 4% y/y, with yield up 29%. Landstar noted elevated heavy-haul demand.\nTotal truck loads are up 7% y/y in October, with revenue per load 29% higher. Both metrics are running “slightly above” historical sequential trends. The company normally sees a slight increase in truck revenue from the third to the fourth quarter as both loads and revenue per load increase approximately 1%.\nSONAR: Outbound Tender Rejection Index (OTRI.USA) for 2026 (blue shaded area), 2025 (yellow line), 2024 (green line) and 2023 (pink line). A proxy for truck capacity, the tender rejection index shows the number of loads being rejected by carriers. Current tender rejections show a tight truckload market. To learn more about SONAR, click here .\nSONAR: National Truckload Index ( linehaul only – NTIL.USA) for 2026 (blue shaded area), 2025 (yellow line), 2024 (green line) and 2023 (pink line). The NTIL is based on an average of booked spot dry van loads from 250,000 lanes. The NTIL is a seven-day moving average of linehaul spot rates excluding fuel. Rates remain higher on a y/y comparison in October.\nLandstar culled its approved carrier list from over 100,000 in 2022 to nearly 68,000 in the recent quarter. The bulk of the reductions occurred in 2023 and into 2024 to combat cargo theft. The decline was also the result of deteriorating TL market conditions, especially for owner operators. Landstar’s approved carrier list was up 5% sequentially in the third quarter.\nTrucks provided by business capacity owners (BCOs) increased 165 units sequentially to 8,709. This was the largest sequential increase since the 2021 third quarter. The BCO truck count is up 22 units from the end of the third quarter. The BCO truck turnover rate improved 480 basis points y/y to 26.6%.\nBCO revenue per mile—Landstar’s preferred metric for TL pricing as it excludes fuel surcharges—was up 23% y/y on dry van loads and up 19% y/y on flatbed shipments. BCO revenue per load was 10.9% higher sequentially in the period.\nLandstar’s BCOs are owner-operators who haul almost exclusively for the company. Changes among this segment of capacity have historically signaled turns in the broader TL market.\nVariable contribution, or net revenue, increased 25% y/y to $212 million. (The calculation measures revenue remaining after purchased transportation expenses and agent commissions are paid.) A variable contribution margin of 14% was 10 bps lower y/y.\nThe adjusted operating margin (as a percentage of variable contribution) declined 220 bps y/y to 30.9%.\nInsurance and claims expenses (as a percentage of BCO revenue) increased 220 bps y/y to 9.4%. The expense line was up $22.3 million y/y (to $55.3 million) due to the aforementioned unfavorable claims items, totaling $21 million. However, Landstar’s accident frequency improved again, dropping to 0.57 DOT reportable accidents per million miles through the first nine months of the year.\nA spike in nuclear verdicts has materially moved the expense line in recent years. In 2016, the company guided insurance expense to just 3.2% of BCO revenue.\nWhy it matters? Landstar’s commentary provides some insight on how the industry is adapting, following the Supreme Court’s broker liability ruling . Its third-quarter results highlight how claims volatility can offset improving freight fundamentals, especially as insurers and brokers continue to adjust to a tougher liability environment.\nMore FreightWaves articles by Todd Maiden:\nWerner Enterprises sees cost headwinds lingering into Q1\nKnight-Swift again eyes big rate increases as new bid season nears\nHeartland Express sees earnings turnaround in Q3\nUpcoming FreightWaves Events\nFreightTech\nF3: Future of Freight Festival\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nOctober 27, 2026 – October 28, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nFreightTech F3: Future of Freight Festival Oct 27 – Oct 28 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now",
